<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1194">1194</a>–<a href="/years/1250">1250</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Stupor mundi, Federico, Friedrich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick II was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 1198, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1212, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> from 1220 and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from 1225 to 1228, the son of Emperor Henry VI of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen dynasty</a> and Constance I of Sicily. In the Kingdom of Sicily he built an early absolutist state run by a secular bureaucracy, and his court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> encouraged the Sicilian School of poetry. Frequently at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, he was excommunicated four times, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> called him the forerunner of the Antichrist. Known as Stupor mundi, he died of dysentery at Castel Fiorentino in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> in 1250, and the House of Hohenstaufen soon came to an end.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor" width="330" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederick_II_and_eagle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
